---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';

const path = '/use-cases/abandoned-cart';
const title = 'Abandoned cart recovery: identity, exits, and app fit';
const desc = 'Plan Shopify cart recovery around channel eligibility, current checkout state, purchase exits, queue ownership, and incremental contribution.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/use-cases" class="text-sm font-bold text-orange-700 hover:text-slate-950">← All use cases</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Abandoned cart recovery</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Recover a checkout only while the invitation still makes sense</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">A cart event is a reason to check eligibility, not an instruction to text. Connect the right shopper to the right checkout, stop when payment or support takes over, and retire reminders that have lost their context.</p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Compare app options</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">Coordinate email and SMS</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Define which abandonment you can actually observe</h2>
      <p>Keep product viewing, adding a variant to a cart, and starting checkout separate. An anonymous cart may have no usable contact identity. A known customer profile does not prove that a newly observed cart belongs to that person, or that either channel is permitted. Record the source event, cart or checkout identifier, event time, customer identifier and the evidence connecting them. Quarantine ambiguous merges rather than attaching a shared phone number to every checkout.</p>
      <p>Choose an explicit recovery unit: one current checkout for a resolved customer, for example. Decide how a new checkout supersedes an old one and how an order on another device closes the outstanding recovery attempt. Avoid sending details from an older cart after the shopper has replaced it. A recovery link should open the intended checkout without exposing another customer's contents.</p>
      <h2>Permission belongs to the channel and the message</h2>
      <p>Keep email eligibility and SMS eligibility separate. A phone entered for delivery, a past order, or email subscription alone should not be your permission rule for promotional SMS. Before admission and again before dispatch, evaluate the recorded permission scope, current suppression state, destination eligibility and applicable sending restrictions. If location or permission is unresolved, pause that channel rather than infer permission from intent to buy.</p>
      <p>Set destination-specific rules with your provider and qualified legal advice. These operational controls do not establish legal compliance. Use the <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent guide</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/tcpa">TCPA guide</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet-hours guide</a> to frame that review. Preserve the scope of a withdrawal; do not equate a channel-specific opt-out with a new permission decision for another channel.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Recheck the checkout before every reminder</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Purchase:</strong> close the matching recovery attempt when an order is placed. Also apply your customer-level purchaser suppression policy across competing recovery flows.</li>
        <li><strong>Payment pending:</strong> pause promotional recovery while a pending payment is resolved. A failed payment belongs to a defined payment-help path; do not silently turn a service issue into a discount sequence.</li>
        <li><strong>Support:</strong> pause when an open ticket or reply indicates an unresolved delivery, billing or product question. Assign a human owner and an explicit resume condition.</li>
        <li><strong>Cart validity:</strong> recheck the exact variant, stock, current price, shipping eligibility and recovery link. A product-level stock flag cannot establish that the selected size is available.</li>
        <li><strong>Age:</strong> expire attempts against a documented freshness policy suited to the catalog. Do not manufacture urgency or promise a reservation that the checkout does not provide.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These are proposed operating rules, not a promise that an app performs them automatically. Confirm which events and send-time checks your integration exposes. If purchase events are delayed or checkout status cannot be read, hold affected sends until the state can be reconciled.</p>
      <h2>One owner for competing workflows</h2>
      <p>Inventory Shopify recovery messages, app flows, browse abandonment, welcome offers and manual support outreach before enabling a replacement. Assign recovery ownership and a shared contact-pressure policy. Give purchase, payment and support state priority over promotional reminders. Choose delays and frequency limits from observed buying cycles and destination restrictions; there is no universal schedule that makes every cart eligible.</p>
      <p>Maintain a recovery ledger keyed by checkout, customer, workflow version and message step. Deduplicate repeated source events and retries against the same dispatch key. Retain event time and receipt time so a late cart event cannot reopen an attempt already closed by a newer purchase. Define a precedence rule for conflicting states, and recheck it immediately before handing a message to the sender. Reconcile uncertain dispatches with provider records before retrying them.</p>
      <h2>Retire queues without replaying stale intent</h2>
      <p>During a migration, stop admission to the old workflow, inventory delayed jobs, cancel or drain them under an assigned owner, and reconcile sends already handed off. Keep suppression and terminal-state records available to the replacement. A rollback should restore configuration without resurrecting completed or expired jobs. After an outage, review each queued attempt against current purchase, permission and inventory state; discard expired work instead of releasing a catch-up burst.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>A conditional app fit</h2>
      <p><a class="link" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a> is a useful candidate when your team wants a checkout-triggered recovery flow and can own the event mapping and filters. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005080447">Shopify data reference</a> describes Checkout Started with a Placed Order filter, and says Shopify's abandoned checkout messages must be disabled manually to avoid duplicate reminders. That makes workflow ownership a concrete selection question, rather than a feature-list tie breaker.</p>
      <p>Its <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005253188">out-of-stock flow guidance</a> also describes configuring dynamic content to skip unavailable items. Check the exact variant behavior in your store. Neither an order filter nor a template condition substitutes for your payment, support, identity and queue controls. Compare <a class="link" href="/apps">other apps</a> against the same requirements if you prefer a separate SMS sender or a simpler recovery setup; confirm current channel, market and integration support before choosing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Hypothetical scenarios: proposed acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>The following are proposed fixtures for a store's recovery setup, not merchant results or claims about a supplier.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Expected operating decision</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>A shared phone appears on two customer records</td><td>Hold the ambiguous attempt; resolve identity before including cart details.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Email is eligible but SMS permission is absent</td><td>Evaluate email independently; do not admit an SMS job.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A purchase arrives before a delayed checkout event</td><td>Keep recovery closed; record the late event without creating another reminder.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A duplicate event arrives during a dispatch retry</td><td>Use the existing dispatch key and reconcile delivery state before another send.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The selected size sells out while another size remains</td><td>Retire or revise the invitation under the availability policy; do not claim the original cart is ready.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Payment is pending and the shopper opens a billing ticket</td><td>Pause recovery and route to support; resume only on an explicit eligible state.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A replacement flow starts while old jobs remain delayed</td><td>Retire old jobs and reconcile handoffs before admitting replacement attempts.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An outage ends after the freshness deadline</td><td>Expire the queued attempt; do not backfill the missed sequence.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Measure contribution against shoppers who could have received the same treatment</h2>
      <p>Assign eligible customers to treatment and holdout before sending, with a persistent assignment across their carts and channels. Keep eligibility, observation windows and purchase matching consistent. Stratify or match on pre-send factors such as cart value, new versus returning customer and prior engagement; do not compare contacted checkout starters with anonymous visitors. Keep other recovery campaigns out of the holdout, and record contamination when that fails.</p>
      <p>For each group, calculate contribution per assigned eligible customer: net merchandise sales after discounts and refunds, less product cost, variable fulfillment, payment, messaging and incremental support costs, divided by assigned customers. Treatment contribution per customer minus holdout contribution per customer estimates the difference for the observed cohort. Account separately for incremental fixed platform and setup costs when judging rollout economics. Do not subtract discounts again if already included in net sales.</p>
      <p>Count matched orders once across providers, wait for the agreed refund window, and report sample size and uncertainty alongside the difference. Track opt-outs, complaints and support contacts as well as orders. Platform-attributed revenue alone cannot establish incremental recovery. If the cohort is too small or other offers contaminate the comparison, call the result inconclusive rather than declaring a winner. Continue with the <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">cost worksheet</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/offer-discipline">offer discipline guide</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
